From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 13/21] dndcp: Drag and drop over Xwayland in a Wayland session

The Wayland backend leaves drag and drop to DnDUIX11. Under a Wayland
session its detection window is an Xwayland client, and the compositor
bridges its XDND to native clients. Several things kept that from
working.

The detection window was override-redirect. A compositor that bridges a
native Wayland drag to an Xwayland window looks for the target among
managed clients, so it never offers the drag to an unmanaged window, and
no XdndEnter arrives. On KWin the lookup is Workspace::findClient(),
called from XwlDropHandler::updateDragTarget(). Keep the window managed,
and keep it from taking focus with _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DND,
_NET_WM_USER_TIME 0 and WM_HINTS input False. Skip taskbar and skip
pager keep it out of the panels.

This is not specific to KWin. On sway 1.11 a native Wayland drag
offering text/uri-list reaches an XdndAware Xwayland window and
completes the handshake. The same window created override-redirect is
offered nothing, and the drag begins and ends without a single
XdndEnter.

A window does not become a drag target the moment it maps. The
compositor has to adopt it and it has to paint, and both happen about
100 ms after MapNotify. The compositor picks the target on pointer
motion and does not look again, and the only motion in this sequence is
the move right after the map. Wait up to 500 ms for the window to appear
in _NET_CLIENT_LIST first. The old code slept 300 microseconds.

The wait polls without running the main loop. It runs from an RPC
handler, and GuestDnDMgr::OnRpcQueryExiting() shows the detection window
before it records the session id. A command dispatched from inside the
wait would run that sequence again, and the outer call would then write
the older session id last. The host's later commands for the real
session would all be rejected, and drag and drop would stay broken until
something reset it. Polling is enough, because the window manager acts
on X requests that have already been flushed.

The detection window is shown twice in a host to guest drop, once to be
positioned and once to start the drag, and only the first may move the
pointer. Moving it again after the drag has started takes the drag off
the target the host chose, just before the button is released. The drag
then ends in GDK_DRAG_CANCEL_NO_TARGET, and the file reaches the staging
directory but not the application it was dropped on. The caller passes
the window's role explicitly rather than inferring it from drag state.

A window manager may place and size a window as it likes. The window was
mapped at the size of the monitor and resized right after, and a
compositor only has to honour the size a window is mapped with. One that
ignores the later resize leaves a detection window over the whole
output, which takes the drop meant for the window underneath. Map it at
the size it is used at, set a size request, which GTK applies on every
map, and give it fixed size hints. sway floats a window with fixed size
hints instead of tiling it. The WM_NORMAL_HINTS also carry the requested
position, and the window is placed before it is mapped, because GNOME
put it somewhere of its own on the first map after vmusr started.

The press that starts a host to guest drag is aimed at the position the
window has, not the one it was asked to take. GTK starts a drag from a
press and motion on the widget itself, so a press anywhere else starts
nothing. After the press the pointer goes back to where the host has it,
rather than waiting for the host's next motion. Otherwise the target
receives XdndEnter after the button has been released, and the drag ends
in XdndLeave instead of a drop.

Warp the X pointer after a uinput move. uinput moves the compositor's
pointer, and under Xwayland an X client learns the real position only
while the pointer is over one of its own surfaces. Without the warp,
XDND picks its target from a stale position.

vmware-user-suid-wrapper opened uinput for the plugin only when
XDG_SESSION_TYPE says Wayland. It now also does so when WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set outside an X11 session, the rule the plugin uses to choose its
backend, so that sway started from a text console gets the uinput
pointer.

With drag and drop in place the backend reports the same capabilities as
the X11 one, DND_CP_CAP_FORMATS_ALL, and hands DnDUIX11 the vmblock
control it uses for copy and paste. Without an X display
DnDUIX11::Init() fails, drag and drop stays off, and DND_CP_CAP_DND is
left out.

dragDetWndX11GTK4.cpp is shared with the X11 backend. In an Xorg session
the detection window keeps upstream's focus handling. It is focusable,
Show() gives it the focus and Raise() calls present(), because Mutter
and the window managers derived from it stack a newly mapped window that
they do not activate below the active one. It gets none of the focus
hints, and there is no wait for the window manager, since an X11 drag
source finds the window in the X server. The size and position hints
apply to both kinds of session.

Tested on Bazzite 44 (Fedora 44) with KDE Plasma 6.7.5 on Wayland,
against VMware Workstation Pro 26H1u1, build 26.0.1.25688693, on Windows
10 Pro 22H2. A 16 MB zip dragged from Dolphin lands on the host, and the
handshake completes in about 110 ms. On GNOME 50.3 (Bluefin 44), a guest
to host drag of text reached Firefox on the host. An Xorg session has
not been tested on a VM.
